---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { repoFile } from "@/lib/site";

const authority = `mesh("<hub peer id>"); subject("<member>"); bound("<member>");
role("member"); issued_at(<ms>); expires_at(<ms>);
audience_unrestricted(true);                 // or: audience("<peer>"); ...
check if bound($k), connection_peer($k);     // only over a link that proved the member
check if mesh($m), root_mesh($m);            // only in the mesh the verifier expects
check if audience_unrestricted(true);        // or: check if audience($k), self_peer($k);
check if time_ms($t), $t < <expires_at>;     // expiry, against the verifier's clock`;

const verifier = `connection_peer("<the peer the transport proved>");
self_peer("<this verifier's own peer id>");
root_mesh("<the mesh this verifier expects>");
time_ms(<this verifier's clock>);
allow if true;`;

const mint = `import { generateSigner, mintToken } from "@statewalker/httpeers-access/issuer";

const signer = await generateSigner();      // { mesh, seed }: mesh is the hub's peer id
const token = await mintToken({
  signer,
  sub: memberPeerId,
  roles: ["member"],
  ttlMs: 60_000,
  audience: [bobPeerId],                    // optional: omit for every peer of the mesh
});`;

const guard = `import { access, ruleSet, withAccess } from "@statewalker/httpeers-access";
import { ANONYMOUS, lookupPeer } from "@statewalker/httpeers-core";

const rules = ruleSet({
  version: 1,
  rules: [
    'capability("app:read")  <- role("member");',
    'capability("app:write") <- role("admin");',
    'role("member")          <- role("admin");',
  ],
  policies: [
    'allow if capability("app:read"), resource("/data")' +
      ' or capability("app:read"), resource($r), $r.starts_with("/data/");',
  ],
});

const guarded = withAccess({
  issuer: hubPeerId,                        // the mesh, and its verifying key
  rules,                                    // required: there is no default
  selfPeer: myPeerId,                       // asserted as self_peer
  provenPeer: (req) => lookupPeer(req) ?? ANONYMOUS,
  revocation: revocationCache,              // optional: the hub's change list
})(handler);`;

const attenuate = `import { Biscuit } from "@statewalker/webrun-biscuit";

// Anyone holding the token can append a block: no key, no call to the hub.
const narrowed = Biscuit.fromBase64(token)
  .attenuate("check if self_peer({bob});\\ncheck if time_ms($t), $t < {until};", {
    params: { bob: bobPeerId, until: Date.now() + 5 * 60_000 },
  })
  .toBase64();`;

const delegate = `// Bob's application issues its own token (its own key), sent in Authorization.
const grant = Biscuit.build(appKey.secretKey,
  'right("/reports/q3", "GET");\\nright("/reports/q3", "PUT");').toBase64();

// Alice narrows it for Carol and hands it over.
const forCarol = Biscuit.fromBase64(grant)
  .attenuate('check if operation("GET");\\ncheck if caller({carol});', { params: { carol } })
  .toBase64();

// Bob's handler: verify against the app's public key, then state what is true now.
const verified = await Biscuit.fromBase64(presented).verifyAsync(appKey.publicKey);
const result = verified.authorize(
  "caller({caller}); operation({op}); resource({path}); time_ms({now});\\n" +
    "allow if right({path}, {op});",
  { params: { caller: String(lookupPeer(req)), op: req.method, path: url.pathname, now: Date.now() } },
);`;
---

<Docs
  title="Access control"
  description="How a peer decides who may call it: Biscuit membership tokens signed by the hub, verified offline against the hub's peer id, bound to the caller the transport proved, and a Datalog policy each peer holds itself. Attenuation, delegation, expiry and revocation."
>
  <h1>Access control</h1>
  <p>
    Every request in a mesh is decided by the peer that receives it. Two inputs meet there: a
    <strong>membership token</strong> the hub signed, which says who the caller is and which roles it holds, and
    the receiving peer's own <strong>rules</strong>, which say what those roles may do here. The token is a
    <a href="https://www.biscuitsec.org/">Biscuit</a>: signed blocks of Datalog facts and checks, verified with a
    public key. Nothing is looked up at a central server, and no secret is shared between peers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The code is <a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-access/README.md")}>httpeers-access</a> (tokens, rules,
    revocation, the <code>withAccess</code> middleware) on <a href={docHref("packages/webrun-biscuit/README.md")}
      >webrun-biscuit</a
    > (Biscuit in pure TypeScript), with the hub in <a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-hub/README.md")}>httpeers-hub</a>.
    This page describes what they do; the <a href="/docs/security">security model</a> describes what the mesh as a
    whole protects, and what it does not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="membership">Invitations and membership</h2>
  <p>
    A mesh is named by its hub's peer id. The hub admits peers with <strong>invitations</strong>:
    <code>hub.invitations.create(roles, ttlMs)</code> returns a 128-bit random id from the platform CSPRNG, valid
    once and until it expires. Whoever holds the id can become a member, so it is a bearer credential; the demos
    show it as a QR code (<a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-qr/README.md")}>httpeers-qr</a>).
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The new peer posts the id to the hub's <code>POST /.well-known/invite</code>. This route and
      <code>/.well-known/presence</code> are the bootstrap routes: they need a caller the transport proved, but no
      token yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      The hub redeems the invitation (a second redemption is refused with 403), records the proven peer as a
      member with the invitation's roles, and answers with a fresh token.
    </li>
    <li>
      From then on the member posts a heartbeat to <code>/.well-known/presence</code>; each answer carries a newly
      minted token, so tokens can be short-lived. A member that stops posting leaves the mesh view within one TTL.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A role exists exactly when some rule fires on it, so the hub refuses an invitation or a member record naming an
    unknown role. <a href="/examples/hub-invitation">Run it: a hub in a tab, and an invitation</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tokens">What a membership token says</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mintToken</code> writes a single <em>authority block</em> and signs it with the hub's Ed25519 key:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={authority} lang="txt" title="the authority block" />
  <CodeBlock code={mint} lang="ts" title="hub side" />
  <p>
    Values are bound as Datalog parameters, never spliced into the source, so a role named
    <code>"); role("admin</code> is a role with a strange name and nothing more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verification">Verified offline, against the hub's peer id</h2>
  <p>
    An Ed25519 peer id inlines its public key, so the mesh id <em>is</em> the root key that verifies the token:
    a verifier recovers it by parsing, with no directory, no key fetch and no network. A hub can only mint tokens
    that self-certify as its own mesh. A token signed by another hub fails the signature check, and an RSA peer id
    (which only hashes its key) yields no key and is denied.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>verifyToken</code> then runs the token's checks against facts that only the <strong>verifier</strong> states,
    never anything read from the token:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={verifier} lang="txt" title="what the verifier asserts" />
  <p>
    The token states what it was granted; the node states what is true right now; Datalog decides. A token is read
    from <code>x-httpeers-token</code> only. <code>Authorization</code> is left to the application.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy">The node's policy</h2>
  <p>
    The token carries roles; each peer derives capabilities from them with its <strong>own</strong> rule set, built
    locally and never fetched, so nothing remote can widen a decision. <code>withAccess</code> puts both steps in
    front of a handler, in the one order that works: verify and bind the token, then evaluate the policy.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={guard} lang="ts" title="peer side" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      The policy sees node facts (<code>operation</code>, <code>resource</code>, <code>self_peer</code>,
      <code>connection_peer</code>, <code>time_ms</code>) and the token's authority facts (<code>subject</code>,
      <code>mesh</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>issued_at</code>, <code>expires_at</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>ruleSet()</code> refuses a rule set that would deny silently: a predicate nothing supplies, a policy naming
      a capability no rule derives, a rule deriving a fact the node or the token states.
    </li>
    <li>Policies run deny first, so a <code>deny</code> beats any <code>allow</code> that also matches.</li>
    <li>
      No usable token is <code>401</code> (a fresh token may help); a token that is valid but not permitted is
      <code>403</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Evaluation is bounded (<code>LIMITS</code>: facts, iterations, time), so a pathological rule set denies instead of hanging.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/examples/access-tokens">Run it: tokens and policy</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="binding">Peer binding</h2>
  <p>
    <code>check if bound($k), connection_peer($k)</code> sits in the signed authority block, and
    <code>connection_peer</code> is what the transport proved: the Noise handshake over libp2p, or the link in the
    examples. A token presented by anyone but its subject fails that check, so a stolen or forwarded token is
    useless to the peer holding it. That is why Carol's token in Alice's hands is refused (<code>401</code>), and why an
    anonymous caller never satisfies a token.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audience">Audience</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mintToken(&#123; audience: [peer, …] &#125;)</code> restricts a token to the peers named. The check joins the
    token's <code>audience</code> facts with the <code>self_peer</code> fact the <em>receiving</em> peer asserts
    about itself, so the destination enforces it, whoever routed the request. Without an audience the token says
    <code>audience_unrestricted(true)</code>, explicitly. A verifier that does not state its own id refuses every
    audience-scoped token.
  </p>

  <h2 id="expiry">Expiry</h2>
  <p>
    The expiry is inlined in the authority block (<code>check if time_ms($t), $t &lt; &lt;expires_at&gt;</code>) and
    checked against the verifier's clock in milliseconds. An expired token is <code>401</code>: the heartbeat gives
    the member a fresh one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="revocation">Revocation and role changes</h2>
  <p>
    A token states its roles until it expires. On the hub, <code>RevocationRegistry.revoke(peer)</code> and
    <code>changeRoles(peer, roles)</code> record a change; tokens minted before it stop verifying. Members hold
    the hub's list in a <code>RevocationCache</code> and pass it to <code>withAccess(&#123; revocation &#125;)</code>.
    Changing a member's roles therefore takes two writes: <code>hub.members.setRoles</code> and
    <code>hub.revocations.changeRoles</code>; without the second, a demoted admin keeps admin for the life of its
    token.
  </p>

  <h2 id="attenuation">Attenuation: narrowing a token</h2>
  <p>
    A Biscuit can be <em>attenuated</em> by whoever holds it: they append a block, signed with a key that travels
    inside the token, with no call to the hub. A block adds checks, and every check of every block must pass, so a
    block can only narrow. It cannot widen: the authority block's checks never see a later block's facts, so an
    appended <code>role("admin")</code> or <code>bound("someone-else")</code> changes nothing.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={attenuate} lang="ts" title="a holder narrows its own token" />
  <p>
    <strong>What an appended block can check, on a membership token.</strong> At verification the verifier asserts
    only <code>connection_peer</code>, <code>self_peer</code>, <code>root_mesh</code> and <code>time_ms</code>, so
    useful narrowings are "only at these peers" (<code>self_peer</code>) and "only until" (<code>time_ms</code>). A
    block that checks anything else, such as <code>operation("GET")</code> or a <code>resource</code>, names a fact
    the verifier never states. It fails closed: the token is refused (<code>unsatisfied-constraint</code>, 403).
    Narrowing by operation or path is done with the node's policy, or with an application token (below).
  </p>
  <p>
    A holder may also <code>seal()</code> a token so that no further block can be appended. webrun-biscuit also
    supports third-party blocks, signed by another key without holding the token; httpeers-access does not use them.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/examples/attenuation">Run it: attenuation and delegation</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="delegation">Passing a right to another peer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Membership tokens are not delegated.</strong> A membership token is bound to its subject (above), and
    no appended block can rebind it, so handing it to another peer gives that peer nothing. Delegation of
    membership is listed as not implemented in httpeers-access, together with per-device binding and key rotation.
    Each peer gets its own membership from the hub, through an invitation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Rights inside an application are.</strong> Because <code>Authorization</code> passes through the mesh
    untouched, an application can issue its own Biscuits, with its own key and its own vocabulary. The holder
    attenuates one and hands it to another member. The receiving handler verifies it against the application's
    public key and asserts the caller the transport proved, so a delegated right can be bound to the peer it was
    given to:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={delegate} lang="ts" title="an application token, delegated" />
  <p>
    In the <a href="/examples/attenuation">live example</a> Carol may GET the report with Alice's narrowed grant, may
    not PUT, and Alice cannot use Carol's copy. The membership token still decides whether Carol may reach Bob at
    all. The application token decides what she may do there.
  </p>

